  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Shower chances into Friday morning, followed by\ndrier conditions Friday.\n\nMajority of the showers were moving across northern New England\nthis evening. These showers were ahead of a cold front just\nabout to enter southern New England as of this writing. It will\ntake just about all night to move this front past our region.\nCurrently thinking it should be over SE MA and perhaps southern\nRI toward daybreak Friday. It could as late as late morning for\nit to completely move past the coastal waters as well.\n\nPWATs are quite anomalous for mid-September, nearing 2+ inches,\nwhich is near the maximum of climatology based on sounding \nclimatology at Chatham. The threat of thunderstorms is low, \nthough not zero. There is also a low threat of localized heavy \ndownpours due to the anomalous moisture in the atmosphere, but \nthese showers are moving at a good pace. Nevertheless, rainfall \namounts will be highly variable given the showery nature. There \nis potential for as much as a half inch of rain in the vicinity \nof Rt. 2 in northern Massachusetts and into southern Vermont and\nsouthern New Hampshire, with much less the farther south you \ngo.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Drier conditions Friday and Saturday.\n\nA few early morning showers are possible across Cape Cod and the \nIslands; otherwise, a transient high pressure system moving out \nof the southern Canadian Prairies brings a dry stretch of \nweather Friday and Saturday. Seasonably mild temperatures are \nexpected Friday, with highs in the middle 70s and elevated dew \npoints initially in the lower 60s, though those fall into the \nupper 40s by Friday afternoon. Breezy NW winds in the afternoon \nwith gusts 20-25 mph, mainly across the interior. Then a shift \nto more fall-like conditions on Saturday. A chilly start for \nmost, with lows in the lower to middle 40s, while urban areas \nremain in the lower 50s. Not out of the question that upper 30s \noccur along the east slopes of the Berkshires and across \nnorthern Worcester County. Otherwise, a crisp afternoon with \nsunshine and highs in the middle to upper 60s. Light east winds \nwill lead to slightly lower temperatures for eastern coastal \ncommunities, with locations like Boston perhaps not climbing out\nof the lower 60s. Dew points in the 40s will add to the crisp \nfeel.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Next chance for rain Sunday into Monday. There \nremains uncertainty in the details such as coverage, timing, \namounts. Showers may linger through early next week.\n\nStill have timing concerns for rainfall Sunday into Monday.\nWhile a high pressure should be moving off the coast Saturday \nnight, it may still have too much influence to prevent rainfall\nfrom overspreading our region during the morning. Still favoring\nrainfall arriving from west to east Sunday afternoon into Sunday\nnight. Another high pressure over eastern Canada should become\nmore of an influence over the course of Monday. Thus, not\ncertain how many showers will remain Monday morning. Thinking\nthe greatest risk for lingering showers will be towards the\nsouth coast. \n\nThe potential rain amounts will depend on the exact track of \nthis low pressure, which is still variable across guidance this\nfar out in time. This variance translates quite clearly to the \nrain totals across both deterministic and ensemble guidance.\nMoisture should not be that much of a limitation. Overall, \nstill looking like another soaking rainfall for most. Favoring \nthe highest rainfall totals towards the south coast of New \nEngland at this time.\n\nThe forecast trend is for drier weather Tuesday and Wednesday.\nIt appears that another low pressure could approach from the\nMid Atlantic states sometime towards Thursday.",
